PRODUCT_RELEASE_PLAN_AR.md §5, Phase 2: "ابنِ أولاً وضع Demo/Import على
corpus ثابت ثم local store... الصفحات المطلوبة: Overview/Health، Incidents،
Timeline، Investigation، Rules، Evidence bundles، Settings، Diagnostics."

Scaffolded desktop/ (Tauri v2 + React/TypeScript, via `npm create tauri-app`).
See docs/product/adr/0001-desktop-shell-and-demo-mode.md for the design
decisions and their reasoning.

Implemented and verified working, real data, both languages:
- Overview/Health: reports observation gaps (system.gap/collector_down) and
  which collector sources are present in the loaded recording. Deliberately
  does NOT claim a live up/down status in Demo mode (no registry.Snapshot
  exists without a running agent) - a different, honestly labelled claim.
- Incidents: full causal-chain rendering, including the causes (solid
  line) / correlates (dashed) / precedes (dotted) visual distinction
  required by §3, confirmed visually in the running app in both languages.
- Timeline: every recorded event, ordered, with severity coloring.
- Investigation (Host): free-text search across subject label/id and attrs,
  confirmed against a real IP address returning its real cross-scenario
  history.
- Settings: language switch (persisted to localStorage).

Data: desktop/src/demo/demo-events.json and demo-incidents.json are a real
export (`netrewind events/incidents -o json`) from a fresh run of
`lab/inject.sh all` on this machine - not hand-written fixtures.

Verification performed:
- `npx tsc --noEmit`: clean.
- `npm run build` (Vite): succeeds.
- `cargo check` then `cargo build` in desktop/src-tauri: both succeed on
  this machine (MSVC toolchain already present; Rust itself was installed
  via `winget install Rustlang.Rustup`, not present before this session).
- Visual verification: ran the real Vite dev server and viewed it through
  an actual browser, in both Arabic (RTL) and English (LTR), across all
  five pages. Caught and fixed a real RTL bug this way - an early CSS
  approach (swapping grid-template-columns + an `order` override) put the
  sidebar on the physical left even in Arabic, which is the LTR convention,
  not the RTL one. Fixed by using logical CSS properties
  (border-inline-end, etc.) throughout and letting `dir="rtl"` on `<html>`
  position the DOM-first sidebar on the "start" (right) side automatically,
  matching real Arabic app convention (Gmail, Slack) - re-verified visually
  after the fix.

Known limitations, stated plainly:
- Rule text (title/why/advice in rules/*.yaml) is English-only; the Arabic
  UI is fully bilingual but incident narrative prose is not yet translated.
  Visible directly in the running app (Arabic chrome, English incident
  text). Translating 19 rule files is separate, larger work.
- No accessibility audit tool (axe or equivalent) was run - not available
  in this environment. Manual checks only: keyboard focus/Enter on nav
  items, semantic headings, logical-property RTL layout.
- The Linux build of this same desktop app (Tauri also targets Linux) was
  NOT attempted in this session. See 13-disk-space-constraint.log for why,
  and for what this means for near-term next steps.
